{"result": {"response": {"id": "gen-1788041311-XMfnnOOBvpsvNTANI53W", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "{\n    \"title\": \"Residential Cabling Standards and Technologies\",\n    \"summary\": \"This community is centered around residential cabling standards, primarily TIA-570, and the associated technologies like UTP and coaxial cables. It details different service grades (Grade 1 and Grade 2) for residential installations, specifying cable types (Cat 5e, Cat 6, Cat 6A), their performance characteristics, and applications such as broadband internet, voice services, and residential automation. The standard also emphasizes installation practices like embedded installations for aesthetic and protective purposes. The primary entities involved are the TIA-570 standard itself, various cabling categories, service types, and installation methods, all interconnected to define best practices for modern residential networks.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"TIA-570 as the foundational standard for residential cabling.\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The TIA-570 standard is the core of this community, providing comprehensive guidelines for residential cabling installations. It dictates requirements for various services, including data, voice, and video, and specifies recommended cable types such as UTP categories 5e, 6, and 6a, alongside coaxial cables [Data: Entities (1153), Relationships (2010, 2011)]. The standard also addresses critical aspects like maximum cable distances, the necessity of power outlets near service outlets, and restrictions on electrical extensions to ensure robust and compliant installations [Data: Entities (1153)]. Furthermore, TIA-570 differentiates its requirements based on the Service Grade, establishing minimum service outlets needed in different rooms, and also distinguishes between single-family and multi-family residences, along with considerations for service entrance and internal distribution [Data: Entities (1153), Relationships (1821, 1822)]. The ANSI TIA 570 standard is a key entity, outlining these guidelines and differentiating between Service Grade 1 and Service Grade 2, with the latter requiring a more robust infrastructure for high-demand services [Data: Entities (1159), Relationships (1831)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"UTP cables and their role in residential data and voice services.\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Unshielded Twisted Pair (UTP) cables are a prevalent technology within this community, extensively used for both data and voice services in residential settings. UTP is characterized by its twisted wire pairs without metallic shielding, making it a cost-effective, flexible, and easily installable solution [Data: Entities (1154)]. For data transmission, Cat 5e is considered the minimum standard, while Cat 6 or higher categories are recommended for enhanced performance, particularly for Grade 2 applications [Data: Entities (1154), Relationships (1826)]. UTP cables support speeds up to 1 Gbps over distances up to 100 meters and are a minimum requirement for data in residential networks [Data: Entities (1096), Relationships (1741)]. They are also recommended for residential automation services, with Cat 5e/6 being specified [Data: Entities (1337), Relationships (2075)]. However, UTP's susceptibility to electromagnetic interference (EMI) and radiofrequency interference (RFI), along with distance limitations in lower categories, are important considerations [Data: Entities (1154)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Categorization of cables (Cat 5e, Cat 6) and their performance for different services.\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The community defines specific performance categories for twisted-pair cabling, such as Cat 5e and Cat 6, which are crucial for meeting the demands of various residential services. Cat 5e operates at a maximum frequency of 100 MHz and offers typical data transfer speeds of 1 Gbps, commonly employed in Gigabit Ethernet and VoIP applications. It serves as the minimum recommended category for both data and voice services, and for basic residential automation systems [Data: Entities (1288)]. Cat 6, on the other hand, supports higher frequencies up to 250 MHz, enabling faster data transmission. It typically delivers 1 Gbps over 100 meters and can achieve 10 Gbps over shorter distances (up to 55 meters), making it suitable for demanding applications and future-proofing networks [Data: Entities (1120)]. Cat 6 is recognized as the minimum recommended UTP cable category for Grade 2 data service and advanced residential automation systems [Data: Entities (1120)]. These categories are classified under broader 'Categorias de Cabeamento' which define performance limits and ensure compatibility and interoperability, though higher categories often involve greater cost and require proper installation [Data: Entities (1615), Relationships (2582, 2588)]. Cat 5e may also be more susceptible to electromagnetic noise, impacting its performance [Data: Entities (1144), Relationships (1805)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Coaxial cable's role in video services and broadband internet.\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Coaxial cable, particularly the RG-6 model, is the recommended cable type for video applications, including television, satellite, and CCTV systems, as specified by the TIA-570 standard [Data: Entities (1333), Relationships (2066)]. The ANSI TIA 570 standard also recognizes 'INTERNET VIA COAXIAL' as a distinct broadband option, signifying its adherence to criteria for high-speed internet access [Data: Entities (1343), Relationships (2080)]. CCTV via coaxial is also a service type distinguished by the ANSI TIA 570 standard [Data: Entities (1342), Relationships (2079)]. This highlights the dual role of coaxial cable in providing both video content and broadband internet services within residential environments under the TIA-570 framework.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Residential Automation and Fiber Optics.\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Residential automation is identified as a service that requires a robust infrastructure, with recommendations for using either UTP cable (Cat 5e/6) or fiber optics [Data: Entities (1338), Relationships (2075)]. Fiber optics themselves are a type of media particularly well-suited for high-quality or long-distance video distribution and play a role in providing these advanced residential automation services [Data: Entities (602), Relationships (2074)]. This indicates that for more demanding or future-oriented residential network applications, higher-performance media like fiber optics are considered alongside advanced UTP categories.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Emphasis on installation practices for aesthetics and protection.\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The TIA-570 standard promotes specific installation practices, such as the 'Padrão de Instalação Embutida' (Embedded Installation Standard), which advocates for structured cabling to be installed within walls, ceilings, or floors [Data: Entities (1352), Relationships (2095)]. This method enhances aesthetics and safety by protecting cables from damage and organizing them, though it involves higher costs and longer installation times, as well as potential difficulties in maintenance access [Data: Entities (1352)]. The embedded installation standard is particularly relevant for new or renovated residential and commercial installations and in environments where aesthetics are important [Data: Entities (1361, 1362), Relationships (2115, 2116)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Distinction between UTP and STP cabling types.\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The community also addresses the distinction between Unshielded Twisted Pair (UTP) and Shielded Twisted Pair (STP) cables. UTP is the more common, economical, flexible, and easier-to-install option, suitable for standard office and residential environments, but it is more susceptible to EMI [Data: Entities (1154, 1616)]. STP, conversely, includes an additional shielding layer for enhanced protection against electromagnetic interference, making it more expensive and complex to install but providing better performance in noisy environments, such as industrial settings or hospitals [Data: Entities (1616), Relationships (2578, 2583)]. The choice between UTP and STP depends on the specific environmental conditions and performance requirements.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 4.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the widespread adoption of these standards in essential residential infrastructure and the potential for performance issues if not implemented correctly, affecting connectivity and services.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null, "reasoning": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "STOP"}}], "created": 1788041311, "model": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1852, "prompt_tokens": 5907, "total_tokens": 7759, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "image_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 0, "video_tokens": 0}, "cost": 0.0013315, "is_byok": false, "cost_details": {"upstream_inference_cost": 0.0013315, "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 0.0005907, "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 0.0007408}}, "formatted_response": null, "provider": "Google", "content": "{\n    \"title\": \"Residential Cabl